ere  you're following the the the actors  aren't blocked ahead of time you're more  just following the action as it's as as  yet no there's no rear so at all I'm  Dallas we would like look with a  viewfinder a little bit and no wet it's  gonna happen but now we're like we don't  know what the actor is gonna do and  everything it's it's tougher for the  focus puller than me I would say but  yeah but what is this it's it's great  because this first idea was because of  an economic economic reason you know we  didn't have time and money for lights  and for Dallas but it becomes kind of an  aesthetics it's variant I still learn  it's because you don't think about it  but as a similar effort you always kind  of control and when you shoot a little  light there's a lot of effects that  happen in the face of the actors that  you're usually if you put already a key  light it arrays it and there's so many  secondary lights that happens in real  life or you know the fact that the  lights coming from window are a lot of  times balanced on green so the light is  greener if you're in the beach it's it's  warmer and things like that and it's fun  to play with that and but I could only  do that with John Mac and with the Alexa  camera I don't trust the other camera  for this kind of shoot and I don't trust  that her director on the job okay to do  that to shoot a little light Brian can  you tell us a little bit about where  this story and these characters came  from sure I did demolition work when I  was probably 17 ty was 21 years old and  I was working for my father mostly these  houses that had burned so I was on the  crew that had to go in and tear the  house down before they started  rebuilding it so there are a couple of  things that happened I learned  you know once you tear everything apart  you tear the walls down you tear the  ceiling down you see the framework of  the house and I remember thinking okay I  get it like this is how it's all put  together I couldn't build it